# The native scan (prefix-fold) kernel (Stage 3t). `scan` was the last comprehension with no
# native form at all: 0.54s against its `reduce` twin's 0.00s at 10M elements, now 0.05s. The
# kernel is SERIAL by definition — element j depends on element j-1 — so unlike the map kernels
# there is no parallel form to keep byte-identical; the order IS the definition.
#
# Every value case prints the FULL output array, because the kernel's one new obligation over a
# reduce is the STORE INDEX — and the classic scan bug is the inclusive/exclusive off-by-one.
# Sabotage-proven: storing the pre-update accumulator turns [0, 1, 3, 6, ...] into
# [0, 0, 1, 3, ...] on the JIT alone, caught by the first line of this file.
#
# The declines matter equally. The guard's operands ([start, end, init] plus captures) are
# consumed whether or not the native path is taken, so a Float init, a Float capture, an array
# source, an array capture, and a shadowed `range` each exercise exactly the stack discipline a
# protocol mistake would corrupt.

# ---- values, full arrays ----

print((0..8).scan(0, (s, x) => s + x))
print((0..5).scan(100, (s, x) => s + x))
print((3..8).scan(0, (s, x) => s + x))

# 20! wraps i64 twice over; the wrap must be the interpreter's
print((1..21).scan(1, (s, x) => s * x).last())

# a branching body (running maximum of a scrambled sequence)
print((0..6).scan(0, (s, x) => if x * 7 % 5 > s then x * 7 % 5 else s))

# captured scalars — one, then two
c = 3
print((0..6).scan(0, (s, x) => s + x * c))
a2 = 2
b5 = 5
print((0..6).scan(0, (s, x) => s + x * a2 + b5))

# a user call in the body, then a call plus a capture
fn dbl(x) = x * 2
print((0..6).scan(0, (s, x) => s + dbl(x)))
print((0..6).scan(0, (s, x) => s + dbl(x) + c))

# wrapping at i64::MAX from the very first element
big = 9223372036854775807
print((0..2).scan(big, (s, x) => s + 1))

# scan feeding the other kernels
print((0..100).scan(0, (s, x) => s + x).reduce(0, (a, y) => a + y))
print((0..5).scan(0, (s, x) => s + x).map(it * 2))

# ---- declines that must still be exact ----

print((0..5).scan(0.0, (s, x) => s + to_float(x)))
f = 2.5
print((0..5).scan(0, (s, x) => s + x * f))
print([5, 1, 4].scan(0, (s, x) => s + x))
arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
print((0..5).scan(0, (s, x) => s + arr[x]))

# a non-tail-recursive callee is not i64-eligible, so the whole scan declines
fn tri(x) = if x <= 0 then 0 else x + tri(x - 1)
print((0..5).scan(0, (s, x) => s + tri(x)))

# ---- degenerate ranges and scope hygiene ----

print((0..0).scan(42, (s, x) => s + x))
print((5..0).scan(42, (s, x) => s + x))
print((7..8).scan(1, (s, x) => s + x))

# init reads the OUTER s, not the accumulator binder of the same name
s = 50
print((0..4).scan(s, (s, x) => s + x))

# the element binder must not leak over an outer variable of the same name
x = 999
print((0..4).scan(0, (s, x) => s + x))
print(x)

# ---- element-wise reads at a size where a wrong store index cannot hide in a sum ----

big_scan = (0..100000).scan(0, (s, x) => s + x)
print(big_scan[0])
print(big_scan[1])
print(big_scan[99998])
print(big_scan[99999])
print(big_scan.count())
